The check is in `assertConfig` in `packages/core/src/lib/utils/assert.ts:96`:

```ts
if (!options.trustHost) {
  return new UntrustedHost(`Host must be trusted. URL was: ${request.url}`)
}
```

This runs before the secret check and the callback URL check, so with `trustHost` falsy every request gets `UntrustedHost`, which matches the INC-2291 outage. The error is defined in `packages/core/src/errors.ts:446`.

The default is set in `packages/core/src/lib/utils/env.ts:44-50`, and the framework adapters set their own defaults (`frameworks-solid-start/src/index.ts:246`, `frameworks-qwik/src/index.ts:366`, `frameworks-sveltekit/src/lib/env.ts:10`).

One thing to check for your setup: in `env.ts` the env value goes through `??` and then `!!`, so any non-empty string counts as true. `AUTH_TRUST_HOST=false` would still enable trustHost. Also, if `AUTH_URL` is set, it takes precedence and forces trustHost on. Neither case is a way to turn it off, so the env var can't be used as a kill switch.

Auth.js reads the secret in one place: `setEnvDefaults` in `packages/core/src/lib/utils/env.ts`.

```ts
if (!config.secret?.length) {
  config.secret = []
  const secret = envObject.AUTH_SECRET
  if (secret) config.secret.push(secret)
  for (const i of [1, 2, 3]) {
    const secret = envObject[`AUTH_SECRET_${i}`]
    if (secret) config.secret.unshift(secret)
  }
}
```

- **Core:** reads `AUTH_SECRET`, plus rotation keys `AUTH_SECRET_1` to `AUTH_SECRET_3`. It only runs when `config.secret` is empty, so your explicit `secret: process.env.ACME_AUTH_SECRET` takes precedence.
- **next-auth:** `packages/next-auth/src/lib/env.ts` does `config.secret ??= process.env.AUTH_SECRET ?? process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET`. It also falls back to the legacy `NEXTAUTH_SECRET`, and an explicit `secret` wins here too.
- **Framework wrappers:** SvelteKit, Qwik, and SolidStart also read `AUTH_SECRET` directly when no secret is passed.

Two things to watch with your ACME_ setup:

1. If `ACME_AUTH_SECRET` is unset, `secret` is `undefined` and Auth.js falls back to `AUTH_SECRET`/`NEXTAUTH_SECRET`. Since nobody sets those, you should get a `MissingSecretError` at startup or first use. That's a fail-closed outcome, but it's worth a test so a misconfigured Vault injection fails loudly.
2. Provider credentials follow the same pattern. Core reads `AUTH_<PROVIDER>_ID`, `_SECRET`, `_ISSUER`, and `_KEY` from the environment whenever a provider is configured without explicit values. If you rely on env-based provider config, those variables will also need to move to the ACME_ namespace, or you should pass them explicitly.
